{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_013bYhiL4MUxEu6kNwRGyN8e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\n2\t#pragma once\n3\t\n4\t// This secret key is used to compute signatures for formulas, clauses, \n5\t// and (un)satisfiability certificates. In principle, in order for the \n6\t// trusted solving procedure to offer guarantees of soundness, the key \n7\t// cannot be revealed to actors outside of the trusted modules. However, \n8\t// if no malicious attacks are being considered, this is only relevant \n9\t// in the sense that the process(es) interacting with trusted modules \n10\t// should not have the particular key in memory (to rule out the \n11\t// possibility of such an actor re-computing a signature). Under this\n12\t// pragmatic view, we just compile a constant key into the trusted\n13\t// modules and keep it at that. When considering malicious attacks,\n14\t// this data should instead be set dynamically for each solving attempt\n15\t// following some secure key exchange procedure.\n16\textern const unsigned char SECRET_KEY[];\n17\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"482b9670-a502-4c42-8c6b-7769943c751b","uuid":"4d9bbc03-c78f-4649-b19c-bbcb9ae890fc","timestamp":"2026-06-05T22:14:01.242Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-31062124/harvest-crust-cL7xON/project/c_src/src/trusted/secret.h","content":"\n#pragma once\n\n// This secret key is used to compute signatures for formulas, clauses, \n// and (un)satisfiability certificates. In principle, in order for the \n// trusted solving procedure to offer guarantees of soundness, the key \n// cannot be revealed to actors outside of the trusted modules. However, \n// if no malicious attacks are being considered, this is only relevant \n// in the sense that the process(es) interacting with trusted modules \n// should not have the particular key in memory (to rule out the \n// possibility of such an actor re-computing a signature). Under this\n// pragmatic view, we just compile a constant key into the trusted\n// modules and keep it at that. When considering malicious attacks,\n// this data should instead be set dynamically for each solving attempt\n// following some secure key exchange procedure.\nextern const unsigned char SECRET_KEY[];\n","numLines":17,"startLine":1,"totalLines":17}}}
